      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers as \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), where \\( a > b \\). We are given two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n\\[ a + b = 6 \\]\n\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n\\[ a^2 - b^2 = 12 \\]\n\nNotice that the second equation is a difference of squares, which can be factored:\n\\[ (a + b)(a - b) = 12 \\]\n\nWe already know from the first equation that \\( a + b = 6 \\), so we can substitute that into the second equation:\n\\[ 6(a - b) = 12 \\]\n\nNow, divide both sides by 6:\n\\[ a - b = 2 \\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\( a - b = \\boxed{2} \\).\n\nTo verify our solution, let's consider the values of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) that make this true. Since \\( a + b = 6 \\) and \\( a - b = 2 \\), one solution could be \\( a = 4 \\) and \\( b = 2 \\), or \\( a = 5 \\) and \\( b = 1 \\). In either case, the positive difference \\( |a - b| \\) is indeed 2.",
