      "text": "$\\textbf{(A)}~1\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}~3\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}~5\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}~6\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}~9$ Let's solve the problem step by step using Python and SymPy.\n\nGiven:\n1. The sum of the two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is 6.\n2. The difference of their squares is 12.\n\nWe can write these equations as:\n\\[ a + b = 6 \\]\n\\[ a^2 - b^2 = 12 \\]\n\nWe can use the difference of squares formula, \\( a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b) \\), to simplify the second equation. Substituting \\(a + b = 6\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ (a + b)(a - b) = 12 \\]\n\\[ 6(a - b) = 12 \\]\n\\[ a - b = 2 \\]\n\nNow we have two equations:\n\\[ a + b = 6 \\]\n\\[ a - b = 2 \\]\n\nWe can solve these equations simultaneously to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Adding the two equations will give us the value of \\(a\\):\n\\[ (a + b) + (a - b) = 6 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 2a = 8 \\]\n\\[ a = 4 \\]\n\nUsing \\(a = 4\\), we can find \\(b\\) by substituting into \\(a + b = 6\\):\n\\[ 4 + b = 6 \\]\n\\[ b = 2 \\]\n\nSo the two numbers are 4 and 2. The positive difference is:\n\\[ |4 - 2| = 2 \\]\n\nLet's verify this with SymPy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = sp.symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = sp.Eq(a + b, 6)\neq2 = sp.Eq(a**2 - b**2, 12)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\n\n# Calculate the positive difference\npositive_difference = max(solution[0][0] - solution[0][1], abs(max(solution[0]) - min(solution[0])))\n\nprint(positive_difference)\n```\n```output\n2\n```\nThe positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
